# Analects Book 7 (述而) — draft, first half: lemmata 0–23 = passages 7.1–7.17. Token format as the pilot:
#   tokens separated by " | "; a token is  CHARS[PUNCT][=pinyin][/context gloss[//char gloss//char gloss]]
#   a leading "^" breaks the verse line before the token.
# Punctuation is editorial (the edition has none) and always trails the word before it.
# Pinyin defaults to the glossary's first reading; every polyphone states its reading here, and so does
# every character not yet in the glossary.
# Passage numbering follows Zhu Xi's chapter breaks (凡三十七章): 7.9 takes in 子於是日哭則不歌 (no ○
# between), 7.10 runs over three units, 7.14 over two.
# Quotation marks balance within every unit (as Book 6); 7.6, whose four clauses Zhu Xi interrupts
# after each, is left unquoted.
# Glyphs kept as the edition prints them: 黙 (= 默), 夣 (= 夢), 脩, 啓 (= 啟), 衞 (= 衛), 顏.
# Readings Zhu Xi marks that modern practice does not keep: 枕 zhèn (7.15, 去聲; we give zhěn); the note
# says so. Kept: 識 zhì (7.2; he allows 如字 shí too), 齊 zhāi (7.12), 馮 píng (7.10), 食 sì and 飯 fàn (7.15),
# 好 hào, 舍 shě, 夫 fú, 復 fù.

UNITS_A = [
# ---------------- 7.1 ----------------
dict(pas="7.1", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^述=shù/(I) hand on, transmit | 而/but | 不/(do) not | 作，/originate | ^信/(I) trust | 而/and | 好=hào/love | 古，/antiquity, the ancients | ^竊=qiè/privately — 'if I may' (a humble word) | 比=bǐ/compare (myself) | 於/to | 我/our | 老彭。」=lǎo péng/Old Peng — a worthy grandee of Shang//old//Peng (a name)",
  l="The Master said: “I hand on and do not originate;\nI trust antiquity and love it;\nI presume to liken myself to our Old Peng.”",
  i="The Master said: “I pass on what has come down to me and make nothing new of my own; I put my faith in the ancients and love them. If I may, I would count myself with our Old Peng.”",
  n="The book is named 述而 from its first words. Zhu Xi heads it: it mostly records the sage's words of self-effacement and of teaching others, and the facts of his bearing and conduct — thirty-seven chapters in all. 述 shù is 'to hand on what is old', 作 'to start something new' (Zhu Xi: 述，傳舊而已；作，則創始也); only a sage can originate, a worthy can transmit. 而 joins the paired verbs: 'but' in the first line, 'and' in the second. 好 is hào 'to love' (Zhu Xi: 去聲). 竊 qiè is literally 'to steal': before a verb of one's own it means 'privately, presuming' — Zhu Xi calls 竊比 a word of deference toward Old Peng, and 我 'our' a word of intimacy. 老彭 Old Peng, Zhu Xi says, was a worthy grandee of Shang, who appears in the Da Dai Liji, probably one who trusted antiquity and transmitted it; He Yan's Bao Xian: a worthy grandee of Yin (= Shang), fond of transmitting ancient things — 'I am like Old Peng, I only transmit.' Zhu Xi: Confucius edited the Odes and the Documents, settled the rites and music, wrote in praise of the Changes and compiled the Spring and Autumn — all handing on what the former kings left, never originating; so he did not dare claim a creator's sagehood, nor even openly attach himself to the worthies of old, his heart growing lower as his virtue grew fuller. Yet by then the work of creation was nearly complete, and the Master gathered the great achievement of all the sages and weighed it: the task was transmission, but its merit was double that of creating."),
# ---------------- 7.2 ----------------
dict(pas="7.2", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^黙=mò/silently (the edition's form of 默) | 而/and | 識=zhì/to keep in mind, remember | 之，/it | ^學/learning | 而/and yet | 不/not | 厭，=yàn/to have one's fill | ^誨/teaching | 人/others | 不/not | 倦，=juàn/to grow weary | ^何/which (of these) | 有/is there | 於/in | 我/me | 哉？」/(rhetorical exclamation)",
  l="The Master said: “Silently to keep it in mind,\nto learn and not have one's fill,\nto teach others and not grow weary —\nwhich of these is there in me?”",
  i="The Master said: “To take things in without a word and hold on to them, to study and never feel I have studied enough, to teach others and never tire of it — which of these can I claim?”",
  n="識 is zhì 'to remember, to keep in mind' (Zhu Xi: 識音志，識，記也), though he allows the ordinary reading shí as well. 黙識 is 'not speaking, yet keeping it in the heart'; Zhu Xi records another explanation, 識 = 'to understand' — grasping it in the heart without words — and judges the first nearer the mark. 黙 is the edition's form of 默. 厭 yàn 'to be sated'; 倦 'to weary'. 何有於我哉 'what of it is there in me?' — Zhu Xi: 'which of these do I have?' These three are not even the summit of sagehood, and still he dared not claim them: modesty on modesty. He Yan's Zheng Xuan reads the line the other way: others have none of these practices; I alone have them."),
# ---------------- 7.3 ----------------
dict(pas="7.3", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^德/virtue | 之/(clause marker) | 不/not | 修，=xiū/being cultivated | ^學/learning | 之/(clause marker) | 不/not | 講，=jiǎng/being gone over, discussed | ^聞/hearing | 義/what is right | 不/not | 能/being able | 徙，=xǐ/to move (to it) | ^不/what is not | 善/good | 不/not | 能/being able | 改，/to change | ^是/these | 吾/my | 憂/worries | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master said: “Virtue not cultivated,\nlearning not gone over,\nhearing what is right and not able to move to it,\nwhat is not good and not able to change it —\nthese are my worries.”",
  i="The Master said: “That I may fail to cultivate virtue, fail to go over what I have learned, hear what is right and not move toward it, have faults and not mend them — these are the things that trouble me.”",
  n="德之不修 'virtue's not being cultivated': 之 between subject and verb turns each clause into a noun phrase, and 是 'these' sums up all four as the subject of 吾憂 'my worries'. Virtue and learning stand first as topics; they are what is (not) cultivated, (not) gone over. 講 jiǎng is 'to go over, to discuss and make clear'; 徙 xǐ 'to shift one's place', here toward the right. Zhu Xi quotes Yin Tun: virtue must be cultivated before it is complete, learning discussed before it is clear; seeing the good and moving to it, correcting faults without grudging — these four are the essentials of renewing oneself daily; if the sage still worried over them, how much more should the learner. He Yan's Kong Anguo: the Master constantly worried over these four. Variant: He Yan's text reads 聞義不能從 'not able to follow it' for 徙, and has 也 after each of the first four clauses."),
# ---------------- 7.4 ----------------
dict(pas="7.4", w="子/the Master | 之/(clause marker) | 燕=yàn/at leisure | 居，/at home | ^申申=shēn shēn/easy, unbent//to stretch out//to stretch out | 如/(the look of) | 也，/(assertion) | ^夭夭=yāo yāo/cheerful, pleasant//gentle//gentle | 如/(the look of) | 也。/(assertion)",
  l="The Master at his leisure:\neasy and unbent he was,\nand cheerful was his look.",
  i="When the Master was at home with nothing to attend to, his bearing was relaxed and his face was pleasant.",
  n="子之燕居 'the Master's being at leisure', made a topic by 之. 燕 yàn is 'at ease, at rest' (Zhu Xi: 燕居，閒暇無事之時, times of leisure with no business in hand). 如 after a description means 'the look of, -like'; the doubled words 申申 and 夭夭 are descriptive (cf. 郁郁 3.14). Zhu Xi quotes Yang Shi: 申申 — his bearing was relaxed (舒); 夭夭 — his countenance was glad (愉). And Master Cheng: here the disciples were good at describing the sage; 申申 did not say it all, so they added 夭夭. People at leisure today, if they are not idle and slack, are too stern; neither fits these words — only the sage has of himself the air of balance and ease (中和之氣). He Yan's Ma Rong: the look of mildness and ease (和舒之貌)."),
# ---------------- 7.5 ----------------
dict(pas="7.5", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^甚=shèn/extreme | 矣/(it has become) | 吾/my | 衰=shuāi/decline | 也！/(exclamation) | ^久/long | 矣/(it has been that) | 吾/I | 不/have not | 復=fù/again | 夣=mèng/dreamed of (the edition's form of 夢) | 見=jiàn/seeing | 周公。」=zhōu gōng/the Duke of Zhou//Zhou (the dynasty)//duke",
  l="The Master said: “Far gone it is, my decline!\nLong it is that I have not again dreamed of seeing the Duke of Zhou.”",
  i="The Master said: “How far I have declined! It is a long time now since I last saw the Duke of Zhou in my dreams.”",
  n="The predicate stands first for emphasis: 甚矣吾衰也 'extreme — my decline', i.e. 'my decline is far gone'; 久矣… likewise, 'long — that I have not…'. 矣 marks a state now reached. 復 fù 'again' (Zhu Xi: 扶又反). 夣 is the edition's form of 夢 'to dream'. 周公 the Duke of Zhou, brother of King Wu of Zhou, regent for the young King Cheng and credited with the Zhou rites and institutions; Lu was his fief. Zhu Xi: in his prime Confucius meant to carry out the Way of the Duke of Zhou, so in his sleep it was as if he saw him; in old age, unable to carry it out, he no longer had that intent, and no longer the dream — so he sighed at how far he had declined. He quotes Master Cheng: the heart that keeps the Way knows no old or young, but in one who would put the Way into practice, the body grows old and declines. He Yan's Kong Anguo says much the same."),
# ---------------- 7.6 ----------------
dict(pas="7.6", w="子/the Master | 曰：/said | ^志/set (your) heart | 於/on | 道，=dào/the Way",
  l="The Master said: Set your heart on the Way,",
  i="The Master said: Set your heart on the Way;",
  n="Four clauses of the same build, verb + 於 'on, in' + object, each followed in Zhu Xi's edition by his comment; he reads them as the order a learner should follow. 志 is where the heart goes (Zhu Xi: 心之所之之謂); 道 is what ought to be done in the daily round of human relations; knowing this and setting the heart on it, one goes straight, without the confusion of byways. He Yan's commentary: 志 is 'to long for' (慕); the Way cannot be embodied, so one sets one's heart on it, that is all."),
dict(w="^據=jù/hold fast | 於/to | 德，/virtue",
  l="hold fast to virtue,",
  i="keep firm hold of virtue;",
  n="據 jù is 'to grasp and keep' (Zhu Xi: 據音倨，執守之意). 德 virtue, for Zhu Xi, is what one has gained in the heart by walking the Way (行道而有得於心 — he plays on 德 and 得 'to gain'); gained and kept without loss, it is one from beginning to end and renewed day by day. He Yan's commentary: 據 is 'to lean on as on a staff' (杖); virtue has a settled form, so it can be leaned on."),
dict(w="^依=yī/rely | 於/on | 仁，/humaneness",
  l="rely on humaneness,",
  i="never depart from humaneness;",
  n="依 is 'not to depart from' (Zhu Xi: 不違之謂). 仁 humaneness is the whole of the heart's virtue once selfish desires are wholly gone; at this stage there is no departing from it even for the space of a meal (cf. 4.5), and everywhere is the flow of Heaven's principle. He Yan's commentary: 依 is 'to lean against' (倚); the work of humaneness reaches others, so it can be leaned on."),
dict(w="^游/roam at ease | 於/in | 藝。/the arts",
  l="roam at ease in the arts.",
  i="and range freely among the arts.",
  n="游 is 'to roam, to take one's pleasure in' (Zhu Xi: 玩物適情之謂). 藝 the arts: the patterns of the rites and music and the methods of archery, charioteering, writing and number (禮樂之文，射御書數之法) — the 'six arts'. Zhu Xi sums up the chapter: in learning nothing comes before setting the intent; set on the Way, the heart rests in what is right; holding to virtue, the Way is gained in the heart and not lost; relying on humaneness, one's virtuous nature is always at work and the pull of things is held off; roaming in the arts, small things are not neglected and both action and rest are nourished. Keep this order of first and last, heavy and light, and root and branch are both covered, inner and outer nourish each other, and one enters the domain of the sages without knowing it. He Yan's commentary: 藝 are the six arts, not things to hold or lean on — hence 'roam'."),
# ---------------- 7.7 ----------------
dict(pas="7.7", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^自/from | 行=xíng/bringing, carrying out (the rite) | 束/a bundle (ten strips) | 脩=xiū/of dried meat | 以/(and) | 上，=shàng/upward | ^吾/I | 未/never | 嘗/once | 無/(was) without | 誨/teaching (for him) | 焉。」=yān/there, for him",
  l="The Master said: “From those who brought a bundle of dried meat upward,\nI have never once been without teaching for them.”",
  i="The Master said: “No one who came to me bringing so much as a bundle of dried meat has ever gone away untaught.”",
  n="自…以上 'from … upward' sets a lowest step on a scale: anyone who brings at least this much. 行 xíng 'to carry out', here to present the gift. 脩 xiū is dried meat, and ten strips make a bundle (Zhu Xi: 脩，脯也，十脡為束); in antiquity a first visit had to bring a gift (贄) as the rite, and a bundle of dried meat was the slightest of them. 未嘗無 'never once without' — two negatives make 'always'. 焉 is 'there, for him'. Zhu Xi: all men are born with the same principle, so the sage wished everyone to enter the good; but where people did not come to learn, there is no rite of going to teach them; so whoever came with the rites was always given something. He Yan's Kong Anguo: if a man could offer the rites, from a bundle of dried meat upward, the Master taught him."),
# ---------------- 7.8 ----------------
dict(pas="7.8", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^不/(if he is) not | 憤=fèn/straining to understand | 不/(I do) not | 啓，=qǐ/open (the way) | ^不/(if he is) not | 悱=fěi/struggling for words | 不/(I do) not | 發。/bring (it) out | ^舉/(if I) hold up | 一/one | 隅=yú/corner | 不/(and he does) not | 以/with | 三/three | 隅=yú/corners | 反，/turn back | ^則/then | 不/(I do) not | 復=fù/(tell it) again | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master said: “Not straining, I do not open;\nnot struggling for words, I do not bring out.\nI hold up one corner, and if he does not turn back with three corners,\nthen I do not tell it again.”",
  i="The Master said: “I do not open the way for a student until he is straining to understand, nor help him to the words until he is struggling to find them. If I show him one corner of a thing and he cannot work out the other three from it, I do not go over it a second time.”",
  n="Classical Chinese often states a condition with no word for 'if': 不憤不啓 'not straining — (I) do not open'. Zhu Xi: 憤 fèn (房粉反) is the heart seeking to get through and not yet able; 悱 fěi (芳匪反) the look of the mouth wanting to speak and not yet able; 啓 is to open up his thinking, 發 to bring out his words. 啓 is the edition's form of 啟. A thing with four corners: hold up one and the other three can be known; 反 is 'to turn back and check one against the other', 復 fù 'to tell again' (Zhu Xi: 扶又反，再告也). Zhu Xi: the chapter before spoke of the sage's untiring teaching, so this is recorded beside it, to urge learners to make the effort that readies them to be taught. He quotes Master Cheng: straining and struggling are sincere intent showing in the face and words; wait till sincerity is full, then tell; and when told, wait for him to get it himself before telling more; and again: open a man before he strains and struggles, and his knowing will not hold fast; open him after, and it comes in a flood. He Yan's Zheng Xuan: if, shown one corner, a man does not think of the rest of its kind, the Master does not teach him again. Variant: He Yan's text reads 舉一隅而示之 'hold up one corner and show it to him' and 則吾不復 'then I do not (teach) again'."),
# ---------------- 7.9 ----------------
dict(pas="7.9", w="子/the Master | 食=shí/ate | 於/at | 有/having | 喪=sāng/mourning | 者/one who | 之/'s | 側，=cè/side | ^未/never | 嘗/once | 飽/ate his fill | 也。/(assertion)",
  l="The Master, eating at the side of one in mourning,\nnever once ate his fill.",
  i="When the Master ate beside someone in mourning, he never ate his fill.",
  n="有喪者 'one who has a death (to mourn)': 喪 sāng, mourning; 者 makes the phrase 'the one who'. 之側 'at the side of'. Zhu Xi: face to face with mourning one grieves and cannot relish food. He Yan's commentary: to eat one's fill beside a grieving mourner is to lack the feeling of pity."),
dict(w="子/the Master | 於/on | 是/such a | 日/day | 哭，=kū/(he had) wailed | ^則/then | 不/(he did) not | 歌。=gē/sing",
  l="On a day the Master had wailed,\nhe did not sing.",
  i="On a day when he had gone to wail with the bereaved, the Master did not sing.",
  n="於是日 'on this day', i.e. on any day when he had wailed. 哭 kū is the formal wailing of mourning; Zhu Xi: 弔哭, wailing on a visit of condolence; within the one day the lingering grief was not forgotten, so he could not, of himself, sing. Zhu Xi prints this line as part of the same chapter, 7.9. He quotes Xie Liangzuo: in these two things the learner can see how right the sage's feelings were; only one who can recognize the sage's feelings and nature can learn the Way. Variant: He Yan's text reads 子於是日也哭, with a pausing 也."),
# ---------------- 7.10 ----------------
dict(pas="7.10", w="子/the Master | 謂/spoke to | 顏淵=yán yuān/Yan Yuan | 曰：「/saying | ^用/(if) employed | 之/(one) | 則/then | 行，=xíng/go forward, act | ^舍=shě/(if) set aside | 之/(one) | 則/then | 藏，=cáng/withdraw, keep hidden | ^惟/only | 我/I | 與=yǔ/and | 爾/you | 有/have | 是/this | 夫！」=fú/(exclamation)",
  l="The Master said to Yan Yuan: “Employed, then go forward;\nset aside, then withdraw —\nonly I and you have this!”",
  i="The Master said to Yan Yuan: “To go out and act when called on, and to withdraw quietly when passed over — only you and I are capable of that.”",
  n="用之…舍之: 之 is the man who is employed or set aside, and each 則 'then' joins a condition to what follows, with no word for 'if'. 舍 is shě 'to let go, leave unused' (Zhu Xi: 上聲); 行 xíng is to go out and act, 藏 cáng 'to store away', to keep oneself hidden. 夫 fú at the end is an exclamation (Zhu Xi: 音扶). Zhu Xi quotes Yin Tun: whether one is used or set aside has nothing to do with oneself; going forward or withdrawing, one is content with what one meets, and fate is not even worth speaking of; Yanzi was close to a sage, so he too could do it. He Yan's Kong Anguo: when it is right to act, act; when it is right to stop, stop; only I and Yan Yuan are alike in this."),
dict(w="子路=zǐ lù/Zilu | 曰：「/said | ^子/you, Master | 行=xíng/(were to) lead | 三軍，=sān jūn/the three armies//three//army | ^則/then | 誰/whom | 與？」=yǔ/(would you have) with (you)",
  l="Zilu said: “If you, Master, led the three armies,\nthen whom would you have with you?”",
  i="Zilu said: “And if you were in command of the three armies, Master, whom would you take with you?”",
  n="子 here is 'you, Master', in direct address. 誰與 is 與誰 'with whom': a question-word object goes before its verb or preposition. 行 xíng 'to set in motion, to lead'. A 軍 'army' was 12,500 men, and a great state had three (Zhu Xi; Kong Anguo the same). Zhu Xi: Zilu, seeing the Master praise Yan Yuan alone, and confident in his own courage, supposed that if the Master led the three armies he would surely take Zilu with him."),
dict(w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^暴=bào/(one who) fights bare-handed | 虎=hǔ/a tiger | 馮=píng/wades | 河，=hé/the River | ^死/dies | 而/and | 無/has no | 悔/regret | 者，/one who | ^吾/I | 不/would not | 與=yǔ/(have) with (me) | 也。/(assertion) | ^必/it must (be) | 也/(pause) | 臨/facing | 事/a task | 而/and | 懼，/fearing | ^好=hào/fond of | 謀/planning | 而/and | 成/bringing (it) off | 者/one who | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master said: “One who fights a tiger bare-handed, wades the River,\nand dies with no regret —\nhim I would not have with me.\nIt must be one who faces a task with fear,\nwho loves to plan and brings it off.”",
  i="The Master said: “A man who would take on a tiger with his bare hands or walk into a river, and die for it without a regret, is not the man I would take. It would have to be someone who goes into an undertaking with due fear, and who likes to lay his plans and see them through.”",
  n="暴 bào is 'to attack bare-handed' and 馮 píng 'to wade' (Zhu Xi: 馮，皮冰反; 暴虎，徒搏；馮河，徒涉 — fighting with no weapon, crossing with no boat; He Yan's Kong Anguo the same); the pair comes from the Ode 'Xiao min' 小旻 (不敢暴虎，不敢馮河). 河 in early texts is the Yellow River. 者 closes the long description: 'one who…'. 吾不與也 answers Zilu's 誰與. 必也…者也 'it must be one who…'. 懼 is, per Zhu Xi, to treat the task with reverence (敬其事); 成 to carry one's plans through (成其謀); 好 hào. Zhu Xi: all of this checks Zilu's courage and teaches him; yet the essentials of leading an army truly lie in nothing else, which Zilu did not know. He quotes Xie Liangzuo: between going forward and withdrawing the sage has no fixed intent and no 'must'; going forward is not coveting office, withdrawing is not keeping one's goodness to oneself; so only Yanzi could share it. Zilu had no greed, but could not be free of fixity, and in asking about the three armies he sank lower; the Master's answer met his fault to save him — without planning nothing succeeds, without fear one is sure to fail; true even of small matters, and how much more of leading the three armies."),
# ---------------- 7.11 ----------------
dict(pas="7.11", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^富/wealth | 而/if | 可/could | 求/be sought | 也，/(pause) | ^雖/even (as) | 執/a holder | 鞭=biān/of the whip | 之/(of) | 士，/a man | ^吾/I | 亦/too | 為=wéi/would do | 之。/it | ^如/if | 不/not | 可/can | 求，/be sought | ^從=cóng/(I) follow | 吾/my | 所/what | 好。」=hào/(I) love",
  l="The Master said: “If wealth could be sought,\neven as a man who holds the whip\nI too would do it.\nIf it cannot be sought,\nI follow what I love.”",
  i="The Master said: “If wealth were a thing a man could get by seeking, I would go after it even if it meant holding a whip for others. Since it is not, I will follow what I love.”",
  n="而 here is 'if' (= 如), as Zhu Xi's paraphrase shows (設言富若可求, 'supposing wealth could be sought'). 執鞭之士 'a man who holds the whip' — Zhu Xi: the work of a lowly man; He Yan's Zheng Xuan: a menial post. 為 wéi 'to do'; 從 cóng; 所好 hào 'what I love'. Zhu Xi: if wealth could be sought, he would not refuse even menial service to get it; but it is fated and not got by seeking, so one rests in what is right — why court disgrace for nothing? He quotes Su Shi: the sage never meant to seek wealth — why ask whether it can be sought? He speaks so only to show that it certainly cannot; and Yang Shi: the gentleman does not hate wealth and rank and refuse to seek them; they lie with Heaven, and there is no way of seeking them. Zheng Xuan puts it otherwise: wealth and rank are not got by seeking, and one should cultivate virtue to get them; but if they could be sought in keeping with the Way, I would take even a menial post. Kong Anguo: what he loves is the way of the ancients. Variant: He Yan's text reads 如不可求者."),
# ---------------- 7.12 ----------------
dict(pas="7.12", w="子/the Master | 之/(clause marker) | 所/what | 慎：/(he) was careful about | ^齊、=zhāi/fasting (before sacrifice) | 戰、/war | 疾。/sickness",
  l="What the Master was careful about:\nfasting, war, sickness.",
  i="The things the Master treated with particular care were the fast before a sacrifice, war, and illness.",
  n="所 + verb is 'what one …': 所慎 'what he was careful about', and 之 makes 子之所慎 one noun phrase. 齊 is zhāi, the fast and purification before a sacrifice (later written 齋; Zhu Xi: 側皆反). Zhu Xi: 齊 means 'to make even' — before a sacrifice one evens out the unsettled thoughts in order to meet the spirits; whether one's sincerity is complete, and whether the spirits accept the offering, is decided here. In war the life and death of the many and the survival of the state are at stake; in sickness one's own life and death. He quotes Yin Tun: the Master was careful in everything; the disciples recorded the greatest things. He Yan's Kong Anguo: these three are what others cannot be careful about, but the Master could."),
# ---------------- 7.13 ----------------
dict(pas="7.13", w="子/the Master | 在/being | 齊=qí/in Qi | 聞/heard | 韶，/the Shao | ^三/(for) three | 月/months | 不/(he did) not | 知=zhī/know | 肉=ròu/meat's | 味。=wèi/taste | ^曰：「/(he) said | 不/(I) did not | 圖=tú/imagine | 為=wéi/the making of | 樂=yuè/music | 之/(clause marker) | 至/reaching | 於/to | 斯/this | 也！」/(exclamation)",
  l="The Master, in Qi, heard the Shao,\nand for three months did not know the taste of meat.\nHe said: “I did not imagine that the making of music could reach this.”",
  i="In Qi the Master heard the Shao, and for three months he did not notice the taste of meat. “I never dreamed,” he said, “that music could be made to reach such a height.”",
  n="齊 here is qí, the state of Qi. 韶 the Shao is the music of Shun, which 3.25 calls perfectly beautiful and perfectly good. Zhu Xi notes that the Shiji has 學之 before 三月 — he studied it for three months; 不知肉味, his heart was wholly in it and reached nothing else. 不圖 'did not expect'; 為樂 wéi yuè 'the making of music', and 之 turns 為樂至於斯 into the object of 圖. Zhu Xi: he did not expect Shun's making of music to reach such beauty, and sighed deeply. He quotes Fan Zuyu: the Shao was perfect in beauty and in goodness, and music can go no further; so he studied it for three months and admired it so — utmost sincerity, deepest feeling. He Yan's Zhou Shenglie: he heard and practised the splendour of the Shao music, so forgot the taste of meat; Wang Su: 為 is 'to make'; he did not expect Shao music to be made 'here' — 斯 'this' being Qi. Variant: He Yan's text reads 聞韶樂 'heard the Shao music'."),
# ---------------- 7.14 ----------------
dict(pas="7.14", w="冉有=rǎn yǒu/Ran You | 曰：「/said | ^夫子=fū zǐ/the Master | 為=wèi/(is) for, supports | 衞=wèi/Wei's (the edition's form of 衛) | 君/lord | 乎？」/(question) | ^子貢=zǐ gòng/Zigong | 曰：「/said | 諾，=nuò/yes, very well | 吾/I | 將=jiāng/am going to | 問/ask | 之。」/him",
  l="Ran You said: “Is the Master for the lord of Wei?”\nZigong said: “Yes — I will ask him.”",
  i="Ran You said: “Is the Master on the side of the lord of Wei?” “Very well,” said Zigong, “I shall ask him.”",
  n="夫子 is 'the Master' as the disciples speak of him among themselves. 為 is wèi 'to be for, to help' (Zhu Xi: 去聲，為猶助也; Kong Anguo the same). 衞 is the edition's form of 衛 Wei. The lord of Wei was Duke Chu, Zhe 輒. Zhu Xi tells the story: Duke Ling had driven out his heir Kuaikui 蒯聵; when the duke died the people of Wei set up Kuaikui's son Zhe; then the state of Jin tried to install Kuaikui, and Zhe resisted his own father. Confucius was living in Wei, and the people of Wei held that Kuaikui had offended against his father while Zhe, the heir's son, should rightly rule — hence Ran You's doubt. (Kong Anguo adds that Jin's Zhao Yang put Kuaikui into Qi 戚 and Wei's Shi Manggu besieged him there.) 諾 nuò is a word of assent (應辭); 將 jiāng 'to be about to'."),
dict(w="入，/(he) went in | 曰：「/said | 伯夷、=bó yí/Boyi | 叔齊=shū qí/Shuqi | 何/what | 人/men | 也？」/(were they) | ^曰：「/(the Master) said | 古/of old | 之/(of) | 賢/worthy | 人/men | 也。」/(they were) | ^曰：「/(Zigong) said | 怨/did (they) resent (it) | 乎？」/(question) | ^曰：「/(the Master) said | 求/(they) sought | 仁/humaneness | 而/and | 得/got | 仁，/humaneness | 又/what more | 何/what | 怨？」/(was there to) resent | ^出，/(he) came out | 曰：「/said | 夫子=fū zǐ/the Master | 不/does not | 為=wèi/support (him) | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="He went in and said: “Boyi and Shuqi — what men were they?”\nHe said: “Worthy men of old.”\nHe said: “Did they resent it?”\nHe said: “They sought humaneness and got humaneness — what then would they resent?”\nHe came out and said: “The Master is not for him.”",
  i="Zigong went in and asked: “What sort of men were Boyi and Shuqi?” “Worthy men of old.” “Did they regret what they had done?” “They sought humaneness and they got it. What was there to regret?” Zigong came out and said: “The Master will not take his side.”",
  n="The speakers are not named; Zigong asks, the Master answers. 伯夷 Boyi and 叔齊 Shuqi (5.22), Zhu Xi tells, were the two sons of the lord of Guzhu 孤竹. Their father's dying command was to make Shuqi his heir; when he died Shuqi yielded to Boyi, and Boyi said, 'It was our father's command,' and fled; Shuqi too would not rule, and fled, and the people set up a middle son. Later, when King Wu marched against Zhou 紂 of Shang, the two held his horse's bridle and remonstrated; once Shang had fallen they were ashamed to eat the grain of Zhou, hid on Mount Shouyang and starved. 怨 here, Zhu Xi says, is like 悔 'to regret'. Because a gentleman living in a state does not find fault with its grandees, still less its ruler, Zigong did not name the lord of Wei but asked about the brothers; the answer made it plain the Master would not help him. Boyi held his father's command highest, Shuqi the bond between brothers; both sought what accorded with Heaven's principle and set the heart at rest, and gave up a state like a worn-out sandal — what was there to regret? Zhe, holding the state and resisting his father, afraid only of losing it, cannot be named in the same breath. He quotes Master Cheng: Boyi and Shuqi yielded the state and fled, remonstrated against the campaign and starved, and never resented or regretted it; the Master held them worthy, so it was clear he would not side with Zhe. He Yan's Kong Anguo: they yielded the state, went far away and died of hunger, so Zigong asked whether they resented it; they took yielding as humaneness — how should they resent it? Zheng Xuan: contending for a state is wicked conduct; the Master held Boyi and Shuqi worthy and humane, so plainly he would not help the lord of Wei. Variant: He Yan's text reads 子曰古之賢人也, naming the speaker, and 又何怨乎."),
# ---------------- 7.15 ----------------
dict(pas="7.15", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^飯=fàn/eating | 疏=shū/coarse | 食=sì/rice | 飲=yǐn/drinking | 水，/water | ^曲=qū/bending | 肱=gōng/(one's) arm | 而/and | 枕=zhěn/pillowing (the head) on | 之，/it | ^樂=lè/joy | 亦/too | 在/lies | 其/their | 中=zhōng/midst | 矣。/(assertion) | ^不/not | 義/right | 而/and yet | 富/rich | 且/and | 貴，/high in rank | ^於/to | 我/me | 如/(is) like | 浮/floating | 雲。」=yún/clouds",
  l="The Master said: “Eating coarse rice, drinking water,\nbending an arm and pillowing my head on it —\njoy too lies in the midst of these.\nNot right, and yet rich and high in rank —\nto me that is like floating clouds.”",
  i="The Master said: “Plain rice to eat, water to drink, a crooked arm for a pillow — there is joy to be found in these as well. Wealth and rank got by wrong means are no more to me than clouds drifting past.”",
  n="飯 fàn is a verb here, 'to eat' (Zhu Xi: 飯，食之也; 符晚反). 疏食 shū sì is 'coarse grain, coarse rice' (Zhu Xi: 麤飯; 食音嗣, cf. 6.9 簞食). 曲肱 'to bend the forearm'; 枕 'to pillow one's head on' — Zhu Xi marks the verb 去聲 zhèn; modern practice reads zhěn, which we give. 樂 lè 'joy' (Zhu Xi: 音洛). 不義而富且貴: 而 'and yet', 且 'and also'. Zhu Xi: the sage's heart is wholly Heaven's principle, so even in the extremity of hardship joy is everywhere present; he looks on wealth and rank that are not right as on floating clouds that are not there at all — nothing in him is moved. He quotes Master Cheng: it is not that he enjoyed coarse food and water, but that even coarse food and water could not change his joy; and again: you must know what it is that is enjoyed. Variant: He Yan's text writes 蔬 'vegetables' for 疏, and Kong Anguo glosses 'vegetable food' (菜食), and 肱 as 'arm'. Zheng Xuan: wealth and rank not got by what is right are to me like floating clouds — not my own."),
# ---------------- 7.16 ----------------
dict(pas="7.16", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^加/add | 我/(to) me | 數=shù/several | 年，/years | ^五/five | 十/ten | 以/so as to | 學/study | 易，=yì/the Changes | ^可/(I) could | 以/thereby | 無/be without | 大=dà/great | 過/faults | 矣。」/(it would be so)",
  l="The Master said: “Add to me several years,\nat fifty to study the Changes,\nand I could be without great faults.”",
  i="The Master said: “Give me a few more years, to study the Changes from fifty on, and I might be free of great faults.”",
  n="加 'to add (to)'; 數 shù 'several'; 易 yì is the Changes, the Book of Changes 易經; 以 'so as to', 可以 'can thereby'. The printed line is hard, and Zhu Xi doubts it. He records that Liu Mianzhi heard Liu Anshi say he had read another text of the Analects with 假 'to grant' for 加 and 卒 'to the end' for 五十 — 加 and 假 confused because they sound alike, 卒 wrongly split into 五 and 十; and he adds that the Shiji has 假我數年，若是，我於易則彬彬矣 'grant me some years, and in the Changes I would be accomplished' — 假 for 加, and no 五十; Confucius was then nearly seventy, so 五十 is surely an error. On that text the line is 'grant me some years to finish studying the Changes'. Zhu Xi: study of the Changes makes clear the principles of good and ill fortune, waxing and waning, advance and retreat, survival and ruin, so one can be without great faults; the sage saw that the Way of the Changes is endless and said this to teach that it must be studied and must not be studied lightly. He Yan's commentary takes 五十 as an age: the Changes exhaust principle and fulfil nature to reach the decree (命); at fifty one knows the decree of Heaven (2.4), and at that age one reads the book that reaches to the decree. Variant: one collation witness (Ruan's reprint) reads 亦 for 易 — 五十以學，亦可以無大過矣 'at fifty to study, and I too could be without great faults', with no Changes at all; this is the reading Lu Deming records for the old Lu text of the Analects (魯讀易為亦)."),
# ---------------- 7.17 ----------------
dict(pas="7.17", w="子/the Master | 所/what | 雅=yǎ/constantly | 言：/spoke of | ^詩、/the Odes | 書、/the Documents | 執/keeping (to) | 禮，/the rites | ^皆/all (these) | 雅=yǎ/constantly | 言/(he) spoke of | 也。/(assertion)",
  l="What the Master constantly spoke of:\nthe Odes, the Documents, keeping the rites —\nall these he constantly spoke of.",
  i="What the Master talked of again and again was the Odes, the Documents and the keeping of the rites: these were his constant themes.",
  n="Zhu Xi: 雅 is 'constant, regular' (常也) and 執 'to keep' (守也). The Odes order the feelings and one's nature, the Documents speak of government, the rites keep one careful of measure and form (節文) — all bear on the realities of the daily round, so he spoke of them constantly; of the rites alone it says 'keep', because they are what a man holds to in practice, not merely recites and expounds. He quotes Master Cheng: this is as far as Confucius's ordinary talk went; human nature and the Way of Heaven could not be heard of (5.12) — the point is to know them silently (7.2); and Xie Liangzuo: this was recorded beside the saying on studying the Changes as of a kind. He Yan's commentary reads 雅言 differently: Kong Anguo takes it as 'correct speech' (正言), and Zheng Xuan says that in reading the canons of the former kings the Master always gave their sounds correctly, so that the meaning came through whole; the rites are not recited, hence 'keep'. On that reading: 'Where the Master used the correct pronunciation was in the Odes, the Documents, and the keeping of the rites.'"),
]

